"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings indicate a Gleason 3+3 (ISUP grade group 1) and pathological T2 stage with no extraprostatic extension, but positive surgical margins. The seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. A key uncertainty is the nodal status, as no lymph nodes were removed, and missing nodal sampling widens the uncertainty regarding regional disease control. The biopsy findings were consistent with the prostatectomy specimen.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of extraprostatic extension. However, the preoperative PSA was elevated at 6.9. The primary factor driving the prognostic uncertainty is the lack of postoperative PSA or follow-up data, which prevents a definitive assessment of the disease trajectory. Missing nodal sampling also contributes to this uncertainty. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trajectory would significantly revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.11–93.68 months; its coverage has not been validated."
